<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hamburger Bank</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1619–1875</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hamburger Bank was a public giro bank founded by the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> in 1619, modelled on the Exchange <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bank-of-amsterdam">Bank of Amsterdam</a>. It kept accounts in a stable unit of account, the mark banco, backed by deposited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, which made Hamburg a leading centre of payments in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. It was absorbed into the new Reichsbank in 1875.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hamburger-bank.jpg" alt="Hamburger Bank" width="320" height="161" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johann Christoph Reteke · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bank-portugaleser_-_Banken_van_Amsterdam,_Neurenberg,_Hamburg,_Veneti%C3%AB,_objectnr_PA_501.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
